    solution: "<h1>Fresh Lunch Box</h1><h2>Problem and customer</h2><p>Students at our college lose up to thirty minutes a day in canteen queues and often eat average food. We serve hostel and day-scholar students who eat on campus and care about time and price.</p><h2>Solution</h2><p>A weekly lunch subscription with two healthy meal options, ordered in advance and collected from a fast lane within five minutes.</p><h2>Business model</h2><p>Students pay 450 rupees a week for five lunches, or 100 rupees for a single meal. We sell through hostel groups and class representatives.</p><h2>Numbers</h2><p>The price is 100 rupees a meal and the cost is 60 rupees, so each meal earns 40 rupees. Fixed costs are 36000 rupees a month, so the break-even is 900 meals a month. At 1200 meals the profit would be 12000 rupees a month.</p><h2>Risks</h2><ul><li>Food safety problems. Response: use a licensed kitchen and follow hygiene rules.</li><li>Fewer students renew after the first week. Response: ask for feedback weekly and improve the menu.</li></ul><h2>Next 90 days</h2><ol><li>Week 1: interview 30 students and test two menus.</li><li>Weeks 2 to 5: run a pilot with 40 subscribers.</li><li>Weeks 6 to 8: improve the menu and measure renewals.</li><li>Weeks 9 to 12: reach 900 meals a month and apply for a small grant.</li></ol>",
